I am not intentionally encoding < to \&lt; . 

I’m using a popular package — the same one used by Ghost. I’ve already attached the code reference in the ticket for your review.

Please refer to the code snippet below. It automatically encodes special characters using the [sanitize-html](https://www.npmjs.com/package/sanitize-html) package and produces the same output I shared in my previous comment.

```js
const sanitizeHtml = require('sanitize-html');

const html = '<b>OWASP < </b>';
console.log(sanitizeHtml(html));

// Output - <b> OWASP &lt; </b>
```

The same output is produced when using [DOMPurify](https://www.npmjs.com/package/dompurify), which is another popular package based on download stats. Please refer to the code snippet below:

```js
import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';
const html = '<b>OWASP < </b>';
alert(DOMPurify.sanitize(html));

// Output - <b> OWASP &lt; </b>
```


Please refer to the following working examples for sanitize-html and DOMPurify:
* sanitize-html ([Go to Code Reference](https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-acemkiab?description=&file=package.json,index.js&title=Express%20Starter)) 
* dompurify ([Go to Code Reference](https://stackblitz.com/edit/vitejs-vite-zigh2oxd?file=src%2Fmain.js&terminal=dev)).


Would like to know your inputs.
